       Some architectures (e.g., PowerPC, ARM) need  64‐bit  arguments  to  be
       aligned  in  a  suitable pair of registers.  On such architectures, the
       call signature of ssyynncc__ffiillee__rraannggee() shown in the SYNOPSIS would force a
       register to be wasted as padding between the _f_d and  _o_f_f_s_e_t  arguments.
       (See  ssyyssccaallll(2) for details.)  Therefore, these architectures define a
       different system call that orders the arguments suitably:

           iinntt ssyynncc__ffiillee__rraannggee22((iinntt _f_d,, uunnssiiggnneedd iinntt _f_l_a_g_s,,
                                ooffff__tt _o_f_f_s_e_t,, ooffff__tt _n_b_y_t_e_s));;

       The behavior of this system call  is  otherwise  exactly  the  same  as
       ssyynncc__ffiillee__rraannggee().

